
def T(t, what, why, how, steps, code, mistakes, qa, exercise, res):
    return dict(t=t, what=what, why=why, how=how, steps=steps, code=code, mistakes=mistakes, qa=qa, exercise=exercise, res=res)

STAGE1 = [
T("Computer and software basics",
  "A computer is a machine that stores data and follows instructions. Hardware is the physical part (CPU, memory, storage, network card). Software is the instructions: the operating system (Windows, macOS, Linux) manages the hardware, and applications (browser, Excel, your Python app) do useful work on top of it.",
  "Every AI system you deploy runs on computers: laptops, servers, containers in the cloud. Knowing what CPU, memory, disk, processes, files and networks are lets you understand errors such as 'out of memory', 'port already in use', 'permission denied' or 'connection refused' instead of guessing.",
  ["CPU: executes instructions. More cores run more work in parallel. GPUs run thousands of small calculations at once, which is why AI training and local model inference use them.",
   "Memory (RAM): fast, temporary space for running programs. When a program needs more than is free, it slows down or crashes.",
   "Storage (SSD/HDD): keeps files permanently. Files live in folders; a path such as /home/asha/project/app.py or C:\\Users\\Asha\\project\\app.py tells the system where.",
   "Operating system: starts programs as processes, gives each its memory, controls file permissions and drivers, and manages users.",
   "Network: computers find each other by IP address (or a domain name resolved by DNS) and talk on ports (443 for HTTPS, 5432 for PostgreSQL, 8000 often for a local API). Data travels in requests and responses under protocols such as HTTP.",
   "Client and server: a client asks (your browser); a server answers (a web server in the cloud). The same machine can be both."],
  ["Open your system monitor (Task Manager on Windows, Activity Monitor on macOS, top/htop on Linux) and find CPU, memory and running processes.",
   "Open a terminal and print your current folder (pwd on macOS/Linux, cd on Windows). List files (ls or dir).",
   "Look up your IP address and ping a website (ping google.com) to see network round-trip time.",
   "Visit https://example.com, open browser DevTools (F12) → Network tab, reload, and read the request URL, method (GET), status (200) and response headers.",
   "Write down, in your own words, what happens between typing a URL and seeing the page (DNS → TCP/TLS connection → HTTP request → server response → browser renders)."],
  "# What happens when your app calls an AI API (conceptual)\n# 1. Your code (a process) builds a JSON request in memory\n# 2. DNS turns api.example.com into an IP address\n# 3. A TLS-encrypted connection opens to port 443\n# 4. An HTTP POST carries the JSON body\n# 5. The server runs the model and returns JSON\n# 6. Your code parses the reply and shows it",
  ["Confusing memory (RAM) with storage (disk).",
   "Ignoring error messages; they almost always name the resource (file, port, permission) that failed.",
   "Running everything as administrator/root, which hides permission problems and is unsafe."],
  [("What is the difference between a process and a program?", "A program is the code stored on disk; a process is a running instance of it in memory with its own ID, memory and resources. One program can run as several processes."),
   ("What happens when you type a URL into a browser?", "The browser resolves the domain with DNS to an IP, opens a TCP connection (with TLS for HTTPS), sends an HTTP request, the server returns a response (HTML, JSON), and the browser renders it, fetching further resources as needed."),
   ("Why do AI workloads use GPUs?", "Neural networks are mostly large matrix multiplications that can be done in parallel; GPUs have thousands of cores designed for exactly that, so they are far faster than CPUs for training and inference.")],
  "Draw a diagram of your laptop calling an AI API: label the process, memory, DNS, port 443, HTTPS request, server and JSON response.",
  [("MDN: How the web works", "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Getting_started/Web_standards/How_the_Web_works"), ("CS50x (free Harvard intro to computer science)", "https://cs50.harvard.edu/x/")]),

T("Python",
  "Python is a general-purpose programming language with simple, readable syntax. It is the main language for AI, data and automation because of its libraries: requests (HTTP), pandas (data), FastAPI (web APIs), and the official SDKs for OpenAI, Anthropic and Google.",
  "Most FDE work is glue code: read data, call an API or model, transform the result, save it, and expose it to users. Python lets you do all of that quickly, and almost every AI example, framework and SDK is published for Python first.",
  ["Python runs your .py file top to bottom through the interpreter (python app.py).",
   "Values have types: int, float, str, bool, None, list, tuple, set, dict. Python works out the type at run time.",
   "Indentation (4 spaces) defines code blocks for if, for, while, def and class.",
   "Functions package logic; modules (files) and packages (folders) organise it; pip installs third-party packages into a virtual environment.",
   "Errors raise exceptions; try/except catches them so you can log, retry or skip a bad record."],
  ["Install Python 3.12+ from python.org and VS Code with the Python extension.",
   "Create a folder lead-tool, open it in VS Code, and run python -m venv .venv then activate it (.venv\\Scripts\\activate on Windows, source .venv/bin/activate on macOS/Linux).",
   "Create leads.csv with columns name,email,budget and a few rows (leave one email empty).",
   "Write the script below, run python clean_leads.py, and check clean_leads.json.",
   "Add a function score(budget) that returns 'hot' if budget ≥ 5000000 else 'warm', and include it in the output.",
   "Save dependencies: pip freeze > requirements.txt."],
  "import csv, json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\ndef load_leads(path: str) -> list[dict]:\n    with open(path, newline='', encoding='utf-8') as f:\n        return list(csv.DictReader(f))\n\ndef is_valid(lead: dict) -> bool:\n    return bool(lead.get('email')) and '@' in lead['email']\n\ndef score(budget: str) -> str:\n    try:\n        return 'hot' if int(budget) >= 5_000_000 else 'warm'\n    except ValueError:\n        return 'unknown'\n\nleads = load_leads('leads.csv')\nclean = [{**l, 'score': score(l['budget'])} for l in leads if is_valid(l)]\nPath('clean_leads.json').write_text(json.dumps(clean, indent=2))\nprint(f'{len(clean)} of {len(leads)} leads kept')",
  ["Installing packages globally instead of in a virtual environment.",
   "Mixing tabs and spaces, causing IndentationError.",
   "Catching every exception with a bare except: and hiding real bugs.",
   "Putting API keys directly in code; use environment variables (os.environ['API_KEY'])."],
  [("What is the difference between a list and a tuple?", "Both are ordered sequences. A list is mutable (you can add, remove, change items); a tuple is immutable. Use tuples for fixed records and as dictionary keys."),
   ("What does a virtual environment solve?", "It isolates each project's installed packages and versions so projects do not conflict, and lets you reproduce the environment from requirements.txt."),
   ("How do you handle a file that may not exist?", "Check with pathlib.Path(p).exists() or wrap open() in try/except FileNotFoundError and handle it (log, create, or skip)."),
   ("What is a list comprehension?", "A compact way to build a list from an iterable with optional filtering, e.g. [x*2 for x in nums if x > 0].")],
  "Extend the script to read an Excel file with pandas, remove duplicate emails, and write a summary count of hot, warm and unknown leads.",
  [("Python tutorial (official)", "https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/"), ("Python venv (official)", "https://docs.python.org/3/library/venv.html"), ("Automate the Boring Stuff (free book)", "https://automatetheboringstuff.com/")]),

T("SQL",
  "SQL (Structured Query Language) reads and changes data in relational databases such as PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQLite. Data lives in tables of rows and columns, linked by keys.",
  "Enterprise data almost always sits in SQL databases. FDEs write queries to understand a customer's data, build features, feed AI with context, check results and create reports. SQL is tested in almost every FDE and data interview.",
  ["CREATE TABLE defines columns and types; PRIMARY KEY makes rows unique; FOREIGN KEY links tables.",
   "INSERT adds rows; UPDATE changes them; DELETE removes them (always with a WHERE clause).",
   "SELECT … FROM … WHERE … GROUP BY … HAVING … ORDER BY … LIMIT is the shape of a read query.",
   "JOINs combine tables: INNER JOIN keeps matching rows; LEFT JOIN keeps all rows from the left table.",
   "CTEs (WITH) name steps; window functions (OVER (PARTITION BY … ORDER BY …)) compute ranks and running totals without collapsing rows.",
   "Indexes make lookups fast; transactions make multi-step changes all-or-nothing."],
  ["Install PostgreSQL or run it with Docker: docker run --name pg -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=secret -p 5432:5432 -d postgres.",
   "Connect with psql or DBeaver and create the tables below.",
   "Insert 10 sample customers and 30 orders.",
   "Write queries: total spend per customer, top 3 customers per city, customers with no orders.",
   "Run EXPLAIN on a slow query, add an index, and compare."],
  "CREATE TABLE customers (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  name TEXT NOT NULL,\n  city TEXT\n);\nCREATE TABLE orders (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  customer_id INT REFERENCES customers(id),\n  amount NUMERIC(10,2),\n  created_at DATE DEFAULT CURRENT_DATE\n);\n\n-- Total spend per customer, highest first\nSELECT c.name, SUM(o.amount) AS total\nFROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id\nGROUP BY c.name ORDER BY total DESC;\n\n-- Top 3 customers per city (window function)\nWITH spend AS (\n  SELECT c.city, c.name, SUM(o.amount) AS total\n  FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id\n  GROUP BY c.city, c.name\n)\nSELECT * FROM (\n  SELECT *, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY city ORDER BY total DESC) AS rnk FROM spend\n) t WHERE rnk <= 3;\n\n-- Customers with no orders\nSELECT c.* FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE o.id IS NULL;",
  ["UPDATE or DELETE without WHERE, changing every row.",
   "Using WHERE to filter aggregates (use HAVING).",
   "Building SQL by joining strings with user input, which allows SQL injection; use parameters.",
   "Selecting * in production queries that only need a few columns."],
  [("Difference between WHERE and HAVING?", "WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after aggregation, e.g. HAVING SUM(amount) > 10000."),
   ("INNER JOIN vs LEFT JOIN?", "INNER JOIN returns only rows with a match in both tables; LEFT JOIN returns every row from the left table and NULLs where the right has no match."),
   ("What is a window function?", "A function computed over a set of related rows (the window) while keeping each row, e.g. RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY city ORDER BY total DESC)."),
   ("How do you prevent SQL injection?", "Use parameterised queries or an ORM so user input is sent as data, never concatenated into SQL text; also validate input and use least-privilege database users.")],
  "Design a schema for a real-estate CRM (agents, leads, properties, visits) and write five business queries such as conversion rate per agent.",
  [("PostgreSQL tutorial (official)", "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial.html"), ("PostgreSQL window functions (official)", "https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/tutorial-window.html"), ("SQLBolt (interactive lessons)", "https://sqlbolt.com/"), ("LeetCode SQL 50", "https://leetcode.com/studyplan/top-sql-50/")]),

T("Git",
  "Git is a version control system that records every change to your project as commits, so you can see history, work on branches, collaborate and undo mistakes. GitHub hosts Git repositories online with pull requests, reviews, issues and CI.",
  "Every professional team uses Git. Customers will review your code through pull requests, CI runs from GitHub, and your portfolio lives on GitHub. Without Git you cannot collaborate or recover safely from mistakes.",
  ["Working directory (your files) → staging area (git add) → repository history (git commit).",
   "A branch is a movable pointer to a line of commits; main is the default branch.",
   "A remote (origin) is the copy on GitHub; push uploads commits and pull downloads and merges them.",
   "A pull request proposes merging a branch, with review and automated checks.",
   ".gitignore lists files Git must not track, such as .env, .venv and node_modules."],
  ["Install Git and set your name and email: git config --global user.name 'Your Name'.",
   "In your project: git init, create .gitignore (.venv/, .env, __pycache__/), git add ., git commit -m 'Initial commit'.",
   "Create an empty repository on GitHub and push: git remote add origin URL; git push -u origin main.",
   "Create a branch: git switch -c feature/scoring; edit code; commit; git push -u origin feature/scoring.",
   "Open a pull request on GitHub, review the diff, merge it, then git switch main && git pull."],
  "git init\necho -e '.venv/\\n.env\\n__pycache__/' > .gitignore\ngit add .\ngit commit -m 'Initial commit: lead cleaner'\ngit branch -M main\ngit remote add origin https://github.com/<you>/lead-tool.git\ngit push -u origin main\n\ngit switch -c feature/scoring\n# ...edit files...\ngit add clean_leads.py\ngit commit -m 'Add lead scoring by budget'\ngit push -u origin feature/scoring\n# open a pull request on GitHub, then after merge:\ngit switch main && git pull",
  ["Committing secrets (.env, API keys). If it happens, revoke and rotate the key; removing it from history is not enough.",
   "Huge commits with vague messages ('fix').",
   "Working directly on main in a team.",
   "Force-pushing over teammates' work."],
  [("What is the difference between git merge and git rebase?", "Merge joins two branches with a merge commit and keeps history as it happened; rebase replays your commits on top of another branch for a linear history. Do not rebase commits others already use."),
   ("How do you resolve a merge conflict?", "Open the conflicted files, choose or combine the changes between the conflict markers, remove the markers, test, then git add and commit."),
   ("What should never be committed?", "Secrets and credentials, local environment folders, build outputs and large data files; list them in .gitignore.")],
  "Publish your lead tool on GitHub with a README (problem, setup, usage, screenshot) and at least three meaningful commits from separate branches.",
  [("Pro Git book (official, free)", "https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2"), ("GitHub Docs: get started", "https://docs.github.com/en/get-started"), ("Learn Git Branching (interactive)", "https://learngitbranching.js.org/")]),

T("APIs",
  "An API (application programming interface) is a defined way for programs to talk. Web APIs accept HTTP requests at URLs (endpoints) and return data, usually JSON. AI models, CRMs, payment systems and messaging platforms are all used through APIs.",
  "An FDE's main job is connecting systems: the customer's CRM, documents and databases to AI models and back. Reading API docs, authenticating, sending requests, handling errors and limits is daily work.",
  ["Endpoint: a URL such as https://api.example.com/v1/leads.",
   "Method: GET (read), POST (create), PUT/PATCH (update), DELETE (remove).",
   "Headers: metadata such as Content-Type: application/json and Authorization: Bearer <token>.",
   "Body: JSON data for POST/PUT/PATCH.",
   "Status codes: 200 OK, 201 Created, 400 bad request, 401 not authenticated, 403 forbidden, 404 not found, 422 validation error, 429 too many requests, 500 server error.",
   "Webhooks reverse the direction: the other system calls your URL when an event happens."],
  ["Read the API's documentation: base URL, authentication, endpoints, limits.",
   "Test a call in Postman or with curl to see the real response.",
   "Store the key in an environment variable or .env file (never in code).",
   "Write a small Python client with timeouts and error handling.",
   "Handle pagination and 429 rate limits with retries and backoff.",
   "Log failures with status code and request ID, without logging secrets."],
  "import os, time, requests\n\nBASE = 'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com'  # free practice API\n\ndef get_json(path: str, retries: int = 3) -> dict | list:\n    for attempt in range(retries):\n        r = requests.get(f'{BASE}{path}', timeout=10,\n                         headers={'Authorization': f\"Bearer {os.environ.get('API_TOKEN', '')}\"})\n        if r.status_code == 429:           # rate limited: wait and retry\n            time.sleep(2 ** attempt)\n            continue\n        r.raise_for_status()               # raises for 4xx/5xx\n        return r.json()\n    raise RuntimeError('Too many retries')\n\nposts = get_json('/posts?userId=1')\nprint(len(posts), 'posts; first title:', posts[0]['title'])\n\n# Create (POST) a record\nr = requests.post(f'{BASE}/posts', json={'title': 'Hello', 'body': 'From FDE', 'userId': 1}, timeout=10)\nprint(r.status_code, r.json())",
  ["No timeout, so a slow API freezes your app.",
   "Retrying non-idempotent POSTs blindly and creating duplicates.",
   "Ignoring pagination and only reading the first page.",
   "Logging full request headers including tokens."],
  [("REST vs GraphQL?", "REST exposes resources at many URLs with fixed response shapes; GraphQL exposes one endpoint where clients query exactly the fields they need. REST is simpler and cache-friendly; GraphQL reduces over- and under-fetching."),
   ("What does HTTP 429 mean and how do you handle it?", "Too many requests: you hit a rate limit. Wait (respect the Retry-After header if present) and retry with exponential backoff; reduce concurrency or batch requests."),
   ("What is idempotency?", "An operation that has the same effect if repeated. GET, PUT and DELETE should be idempotent; for POST, use idempotency keys so retries do not create duplicates."),
   ("How do you secure a webhook endpoint?", "Use HTTPS, verify the sender's signature with the shared secret, check timestamps to block replays, and respond quickly while processing in the background.")],
  "Build a script that reads a public API page by page, stores the results in SQLite, and re-runs safely without duplicates.",
  [("MDN: HTTP overview", "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Overview"), ("Requests library docs", "https://requests.readthedocs.io/en/latest/"), ("Postman learning center", "https://learning.postman.com/docs/introduction/overview/")]),

T("JavaScript basics",
  "JavaScript is the programming language of web browsers and, through Node.js, of many servers. It makes pages interactive and is the base of React, Next.js and TypeScript.",
  "Most AI products have a web front end. Even backend-focused FDEs read and adjust JavaScript/TypeScript to fix a UI, add a chat widget, call an API from the browser or debug what a customer sees.",
  ["Variables: const (cannot be reassigned) and let. Types: string, number, boolean, null, undefined, object, array.",
   "Functions: function f(x) {} or arrow functions const f = (x) => x * 2.",
   "Arrays and objects: [1,2,3].map(), .filter(), .find(); {name: 'Asha'} with dot access.",
   "The DOM is the page as objects; document.querySelector finds elements and addEventListener reacts to clicks.",
   "Async: fetch returns a Promise; await waits for it inside an async function.",
   "Modules: export and import share code between files."],
  ["Install Node.js LTS and check node -v and npm -v.",
   "Create index.html with an input, a button and a list.",
   "Add a script that fetches posts from a practice API and shows the titles.",
   "Open DevTools Console to see errors and console.log output.",
   "Move the logic into app.js and load it with <script type=\"module\" src=\"app.js\">."],
  "<!-- index.html -->\n<input id=\"q\" placeholder=\"Filter titles\">\n<button id=\"go\">Load</button>\n<ul id=\"list\"></ul>\n<script type=\"module\">\n  const list = document.querySelector('#list');\n  document.querySelector('#go').addEventListener('click', async () => {\n    list.textContent = 'Loading…';\n    try {\n      const res = await fetch('https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts');\n      if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${res.status}`);\n      const posts = await res.json();\n      const q = document.querySelector('#q').value.toLowerCase();\n      list.innerHTML = '';\n      posts.filter(p => p.title.includes(q)).slice(0, 10).forEach(p => {\n        const li = document.createElement('li');\n        li.textContent = p.title;   // textContent avoids HTML injection\n        list.append(li);\n      });\n    } catch (err) {\n      list.textContent = 'Could not load: ' + err.message;\n    }\n  });\n</script>",
  ["Using == instead of === (loose comparison causes surprises).",
   "Forgetting await, so you get a Promise instead of data.",
   "Inserting user text with innerHTML, which allows script injection (XSS).",
   "Putting secret API keys in browser code, where anyone can read them."],
  [("let vs const vs var?", "const cannot be reassigned, let can; both are block-scoped. var is function-scoped and hoisted, so modern code avoids it."),
   ("What is a Promise?", "An object representing a value that will be available later (or an error). You consume it with .then/.catch or await inside an async function."),
   ("Why must API keys not be in frontend code?", "Everything shipped to the browser can be read by users; keys must stay on the server, which calls the API on the user's behalf.")],
  "Build a small page that calls your own backend endpoint and shows a loading state, results and an error message.",
  [("MDN JavaScript guide", "https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/JavaScript/Guide"), ("javascript.info (free)", "https://javascript.info/"), ("Node.js learn", "https://nodejs.org/en/learn")]),

T("Terminal",
  "The terminal (command line, shell) is a text interface for running programs and managing files: bash or zsh on macOS/Linux, PowerShell on Windows. Servers, containers and cloud tools are mostly operated from it.",
  "Installing packages, running servers, using Git, Docker, kubectl and cloud CLIs, reading logs on a server and automating tasks all happen in the terminal. Customers' production servers rarely have a graphical interface.",
  ["The prompt waits for a command; a command has a name, options (flags) and arguments: ls -la /var/log.",
   "Navigation: pwd (where am I), ls (list), cd (change folder), mkdir, rm, cp, mv.",
   "Reading files: cat, less, head, tail -f (follow a log), grep (search text).",
   "Environment variables: export API_KEY=… (macOS/Linux) or $env:API_KEY='…' (PowerShell).",
   "Pipes (|) send one command's output into another; > writes output to a file.",
   "Processes: ps, top, kill; Ctrl+C stops the running command."],
  ["Open a terminal and run pwd, ls, mkdir demo, cd demo.",
   "Create a file: echo 'hello' > note.txt; read it with cat note.txt.",
   "Search: grep -i error app.log | tail -n 20.",
   "Set an environment variable and read it in Python with os.environ.",
   "Run a local server (python -m http.server 8000) and open http://localhost:8000; stop it with Ctrl+C.",
   "Find what is using a port: lsof -i :8000 (macOS/Linux) or netstat -ano | findstr 8000 (Windows)."],
  "# macOS / Linux (bash or zsh)\npwd                      # print working directory\nls -la                   # list all files with details\nmkdir -p projects/fde && cd projects/fde\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install requests\nexport API_TOKEN='paste-token-here'   # set for this session only\ngrep -n 'ERROR' app.log | tail -n 20 # last 20 error lines\ntail -f app.log          # follow a log live (Ctrl+C to stop)\ncurl -s https://api.github.com | head -n 5\n\n# Windows PowerShell equivalents\n# Get-Location; Get-ChildItem; Set-Location projects\\fde\n# python -m venv .venv; .venv\\Scripts\\Activate.ps1\n# $env:API_TOKEN = 'paste-token-here'\n# Select-String -Path app.log -Pattern 'ERROR' | Select-Object -Last 20",
  ["Running rm -rf or deleting without checking the path.",
   "Pasting commands from the internet without understanding them.",
   "Saving secrets in shell history; prefer .env files loaded by the app.",
   "Forgetting to activate the virtual environment."],
  [("How do you find and stop a process using port 8000?", "Find its PID with lsof -i :8000 (or netstat on Windows), then stop it with kill <PID> (or Stop-Process)."),
   ("What does a pipe do?", "It sends the output of one command as input to the next, e.g. cat app.log | grep ERROR | wc -l counts error lines."),
   ("How do you follow a log file as it grows?", "tail -f filename (Get-Content -Wait on PowerShell).")],
  "Write a shell script setup.sh that creates a virtual environment, installs requirements and starts your API; document it in the README.",
  [("The Missing Semester (MIT, free)", "https://missing.csail.mit.edu/"), ("Microsoft: PowerShell docs", "https://learn.microsoft.com/powershell/scripting/overview"), ("explainshell (explains commands)", "https://explainshell.com/")]),

T("Debugging",
  "Debugging is the systematic process of finding why software behaves wrongly and fixing the cause. It uses error messages, logs, breakpoints, tests and experiments.",
  "In customer deployments things break in ways that never happened on your laptop: different data, permissions, networks and versions. A calm, systematic debugging method is what makes an FDE trusted.",
  ["Reproduce: get the bug to happen reliably with the smallest input.",
   "Read the error: the last line of a Python traceback names the exception; the lines above show where it happened.",
   "Form a hypothesis and test one change at a time.",
   "Inspect state: print/log variables or set breakpoints in a debugger and step through.",
   "Isolate: remove parts until the bug disappears; binary-search through recent changes (git bisect).",
   "Fix the cause, add a test that would have caught it, and confirm the fix."],
  ["Run the failing script and copy the full traceback.",
   "Find the top line inside your own code and open that file and line.",
   "In VS Code, click left of the line number to set a breakpoint and press F5 (Run and Debug).",
   "Step over (F10) and into (F11); watch variables in the left panel.",
   "When the bad value appears, ask why it was produced; fix that.",
   "Write a pytest test for the case and run pytest."],
  "import logging\nlogging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO, format='%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(message)s')\nlog = logging.getLogger('leads')\n\ndef average_budget(leads: list[dict]) -> float:\n    budgets = []\n    for lead in leads:\n        try:\n            budgets.append(int(lead['budget']))\n        except (KeyError, ValueError) as e:\n            log.warning('Skipping lead %s: bad budget %r (%s)', lead.get('name'), lead.get('budget'), e)\n    if not budgets:                 # bug fixed: division by zero on empty input\n        return 0.0\n    return sum(budgets) / len(budgets)\n\n# test_leads.py  (run: pytest)\ndef test_average_ignores_bad_rows():\n    assert average_budget([{'name': 'A', 'budget': '100'}, {'name': 'B', 'budget': 'n/a'}]) == 100\n\ndef test_average_empty():\n    assert average_budget([]) == 0.0",
  ["Changing many things at once, so you do not know what fixed it.",
   "Fixing the symptom (catching and ignoring the error) instead of the cause.",
   "Not reading the whole error message.",
   "Debugging in production without logs or a way to reproduce."],
  [("Walk me through how you debug a production issue.", "Assess impact and mitigate first (rollback or feature flag), gather evidence (logs, metrics, traces, recent changes), reproduce in a safe environment, form and test hypotheses, fix the root cause, add a test and monitoring, and write a short post-mortem."),
   ("What is a stack trace?", "The list of function calls active when an error occurred, from the entry point to the failing line; it shows where and how the program got there."),
   ("How do you debug an LLM feature that gives bad answers?", "Log the exact prompt, context, model and parameters; reproduce with the same inputs; check whether retrieval returned the right documents; vary one thing (prompt, context, model) at a time; and add the case to the evaluation set.")],
  "Take a script with three planted bugs (wrong key, off-by-one, empty input) and find each using the debugger, then add a test for each.",
  [("VS Code: Python debugging", "https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/python/debugging"), ("Python logging HOWTO (official)", "https://docs.python.org/3/howto/logging.html"), ("pytest documentation", "https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/")]),
]

STAGE2 = [
T("OpenAI API",
  "The OpenAI API gives programmatic access to OpenAI's GPT models for text, reasoning, vision, audio, embeddings and tool calling. The current main endpoint is the Responses API; the older Chat Completions API is still widely used in existing code.",
  "Many enterprise customers already use OpenAI models, often through Azure OpenAI. An FDE must be able to call the API, control output format, use tools, handle errors and estimate cost.",
  ["Create an API key in the OpenAI platform and keep it in the OPENAI_API_KEY environment variable.",
   "Install the SDK (pip install openai) and create a client: client = OpenAI().",
   "Send input (text or a list of messages with roles) and a model name; read the text from the response.",
   "Use instructions for system-level rules, tools for function calling, and structured outputs (JSON schema) for reliable data.",
   "Usage fields report input and output tokens; cost = tokens × the published price per million tokens.",
   "Choose a model from the official models page; names change over time."],
  ["Create an account and API key; set a monthly spending limit.",
   "pip install openai; export OPENAI_API_KEY=… (never commit it).",
   "Run the basic example below and print the answer and usage.",
   "Add an instruction that sets tone and format.",
   "Ask for JSON output and validate it with json.loads or Pydantic.",
   "Wrap the call with try/except for rate limits and API errors."],
  "# pip install openai\nfrom openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()  # reads OPENAI_API_KEY from the environment\nMODEL = 'MODEL_ID'  # pick a current model from https://platform.openai.com/docs/models\n\nresp = client.responses.create(\n    model=MODEL,\n    instructions='You are a concise real-estate assistant. Answer in 3 bullet points.',\n    input='What should a first-time buyer check before booking a flat?',\n)\nprint(resp.output_text)\nprint(resp.usage)  # input/output token counts for cost tracking",
  ["Hard-coding the key in source code or frontend code.",
   "Not setting a spending limit during learning.",
   "Parsing free text when you need structured data; use structured outputs.",
   "Assuming a model name from an old tutorial still exists."],
  [("How do you control cost when calling an LLM API?", "Pick the smallest model that meets quality on your evaluation set, keep prompts and context short, cap output length, cache repeated prompts or answers, batch offline jobs, and monitor tokens per request."),
   ("Responses API vs Chat Completions?", "Both generate model output. Responses is OpenAI's newer, unified interface with built-in tools and state options; Chat Completions takes a messages list and is common in older code. Check the current docs for which features each supports."),
   ("How do you get reliable JSON from a model?", "Use the provider's structured-output feature with a JSON schema, validate the result in code, and retry or fall back when validation fails.")],
  "Build a command-line tool that takes a property description and returns JSON with price_band, highlights and risks, validated with Pydantic.",
  [("OpenAI API docs (official)", "https://platform.openai.com/docs"), ("OpenAI: text generation guide", "https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/text"), ("OpenAI: structured outputs", "https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/structured-outputs")]),

T("Claude API (Anthropic)",
  "The Claude API gives access to Anthropic's Claude models through the Messages API. Claude is used for long documents, coding, analysis and agentic tool use. Current models include Claude Opus 5.5 (claude-opus-5-5, the default), Claude Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) and Claude Haiku 5.5 (claude-haiku-5-5).",
  "Claude is widely used in enterprise for document-heavy and agent workloads, and is available directly, on Amazon Bedrock, Google Cloud Vertex AI and Microsoft Foundry, which matters when customers require their own cloud.",
  ["Install the SDK (pip install anthropic); the client reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from the environment.",
   "Every call goes to messages.create with model, max_tokens, an optional system prompt and a list of messages (user/assistant).",
   "The response contains content blocks (text, tool_use) and a stop_reason (end_turn, max_tokens, tool_use, refusal).",
   "Tools are defined with name, description and input_schema; Claude returns tool_use blocks that your code executes and answers with tool_result blocks.",
   "messages.count_tokens estimates input tokens before sending; usage on the response reports what was billed.",
   "Effort (output_config.effort: low to max) trades depth of reasoning for speed and cost; prompt caching cuts cost for repeated long prefixes."],
  ["Create a key in the Claude Console; set a spend limit.",
   "pip install anthropic; export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=…",
   "Run the basic call below.",
   "Check stop_reason before using the text.",
   "Add a tool and run the loop: call → execute tool → return result → repeat until end_turn.",
   "Count tokens on a long document before sending it."],
  "# pip install anthropic\nimport anthropic\n\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()  # reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY\n\nmsg = client.messages.create(\n    model='claude-opus-5-5',\n    max_tokens=2000,\n    system='You are a concise real-estate assistant. Answer in 3 bullet points.',\n    messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': 'What should a first-time buyer check before booking a flat?'}],\n)\nif msg.stop_reason == 'refusal':\n    print('Request declined')\nelse:\n    print(''.join(b.text for b in msg.content if b.type == 'text'))\nprint(msg.usage.input_tokens, msg.usage.output_tokens)\n\n# Count tokens before sending a long document\ncount = client.messages.count_tokens(\n    model='claude-opus-5-5',\n    messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': open('policy.txt', encoding='utf-8').read()}],\n)\nprint('input tokens:', count.input_tokens)",
  ["Reading msg.content[0].text without checking block type or stop_reason.",
   "Setting max_tokens too low, which cuts answers off (stop_reason = max_tokens).",
   "Dropping earlier assistant content blocks when continuing a tool-use conversation.",
   "Using model IDs from old tutorials; use the IDs listed in the official models page."],
  [("How does tool use work in the Claude API?", "You send tool definitions with JSON schemas. When Claude needs a tool it returns stop_reason tool_use with tool_use blocks (name, input). Your code runs the tool and sends a user message with tool_result blocks referencing the tool_use IDs. You repeat until stop_reason is end_turn."),
   ("How would you reduce cost for a chatbot that sends the same long policy document every call?", "Use prompt caching on the stable prefix (system prompt and document), keep the volatile question after it, choose an appropriate model and effort, and verify cache hits in usage."),
   ("A customer needs data to stay in their AWS account. Options?", "Use Claude through Amazon Bedrock (or Claude Platform on AWS) in their account and region, with their IAM, logging and network controls.")],
  "Build a document Q&A script: load a PDF policy, send it to Claude with a question, and return the answer with quoted supporting sentences.",
  [("Claude API documentation (official)", "https://docs.claude.com/"), ("Claude: tool use overview", "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview"), ("Anthropic Python SDK", "https://github.com/anthropics/anthropic-sdk-python")]),

T("Gemini API (Google)",
  "The Gemini API gives access to Google's Gemini models for text, images, audio, video and long documents. Developers use it through Google AI Studio with an API key, or through Vertex AI on Google Cloud for enterprise controls.",
  "Customers on Google Cloud and Google Workspace often prefer Gemini on Vertex AI. Gemini's multimodal and long-context features are useful for video, audio and large document workloads.",
  ["Get a key in Google AI Studio (development) or use a Google Cloud project with Vertex AI (enterprise).",
   "Install the Google Gen AI SDK: pip install google-genai.",
   "Create a client and call models.generate_content with a model name and contents.",
   "Configure system instructions, temperature, safety settings and structured JSON output through a config object.",
   "Function calling and grounding with Google Search are available as tools.",
   "Choose a current model name from the official models page."],
  ["Create an API key in Google AI Studio; set GEMINI_API_KEY.",
   "pip install google-genai.",
   "Run the basic example and print the text.",
   "Add a system instruction and a JSON response schema.",
   "Try a multimodal call with an image file.",
   "For production on Google Cloud, switch the client to Vertex AI with your project and region."],
  "# pip install google-genai\nfrom google import genai\nfrom google.genai import types\n\nclient = genai.Client()  # reads GEMINI_API_KEY (or GOOGLE_API_KEY)\nMODEL = 'MODEL_ID'  # pick a current model from https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/models\n\nresp = client.models.generate_content(\n    model=MODEL,\n    contents='What should a first-time buyer check before booking a flat?',\n    config=types.GenerateContentConfig(\n        system_instruction='You are a concise real-estate assistant. Answer in 3 bullet points.',\n    ),\n)\nprint(resp.text)",
  ["Mixing the AI Studio and Vertex AI setups (keys vs Google Cloud credentials).",
   "Ignoring safety settings and finish reasons when output is blocked.",
   "Uploading sensitive customer files to a personal developer account."],
  [("When would you choose Vertex AI over AI Studio?", "For enterprise use: Google Cloud IAM, VPC controls, data residency, audit logging, quotas and billing under the customer's project. AI Studio is for quick development and prototypes."),
   ("Why build a multi-provider abstraction?", "To compare quality and cost, meet customer cloud requirements, and fail over during outages without rewriting the application.")],
  "Write one function ask(provider, prompt) that calls OpenAI, Claude or Gemini and returns plain text, then compare their answers and token costs on five prompts.",
  [("Gemini API docs (official)", "https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs"), ("Google Gen AI Python SDK", "https://googleapis.github.io/python-genai/"), ("Vertex AI generative AI docs", "https://cloud.google.com/vertex-ai/generative-ai/docs")]),

T("Prompt engineering",
  "Prompt engineering is designing the instructions, examples and format requests you give a model so it produces correct, consistent and useful output for a task.",
  "Most quality problems in early AI deployments are prompt problems: vague instructions, missing context, no examples, no output format. Good prompts are cheap to change and often give the biggest quality gain.",
  ["Be clear and direct: say the task, the audience, the constraints and the output format.",
   "Give context: who the user is, what the data is, what good looks like.",
   "Use examples (few-shot) to show the exact pattern you want.",
   "Separate instructions from data with tags such as <document>…</document> so the model does not confuse them.",
   "Ask for structured output (JSON with named fields) when code will read it.",
   "Test prompts against a fixed set of real cases and change one thing at a time."],
  ["Write the task in one sentence and list what a perfect answer contains.",
   "Collect 20 real inputs with expected outputs.",
   "Write version 1 of the prompt with role, task, rules, format and two examples.",
   "Run all 20 cases and score them.",
   "Fix the most common failure, re-run, and keep the version that scores better.",
   "Store prompts in files with version numbers."],
  "SYSTEM = '''You classify customer enquiries for a real-estate agency.\nReturn only JSON: {\"intent\": one of [\"buy\", \"rent\", \"sell\", \"other\"],\n\"budget_inr\": number or null, \"location\": string or null, \"urgency\": \"high\"|\"normal\"}.\nIf a field is not stated, use null. Do not guess numbers.'''\n\nEXAMPLES = '''<example>\n<enquiry>Looking for a 2BHK to buy in Whitefield, around 90 lakhs, need it this month</enquiry>\n<json>{\"intent\":\"buy\",\"budget_inr\":9000000,\"location\":\"Whitefield\",\"urgency\":\"high\"}</json>\n</example>'''\n\ndef build_prompt(enquiry: str) -> str:\n    return f'{EXAMPLES}\\n<enquiry>{enquiry}</enquiry>'",
  ["Vague tasks ('summarise this') without audience, length or focus.",
   "Hiding rules deep inside long text.",
   "Never testing on real cases.",
   "Putting untrusted user text where it can be read as instructions."],
  [("How do you make outputs consistent?", "Give explicit format rules, examples, a schema or structured-output feature, a lower temperature where available, and validate outputs in code."),
   ("What is few-shot prompting and when does it help?", "Including worked examples of input and desired output in the prompt; it helps when format or judgement is hard to describe in words."),
   ("How do you know a prompt change is an improvement?", "Run both versions on the same evaluation set and compare scores, not on one or two hand-picked examples.")],
  "Create a prompt that extracts invoice number, date, vendor and total from invoice text, test it on 15 invoices and report the accuracy per field.",
  [("Claude: prompt engineering overview", "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview"), ("OpenAI: prompting guide", "https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/prompt-engineering"), ("Google: prompt design strategies", "https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/prompting-strategies")]),

T("Context engineering",
  "Context engineering is deciding what information the model sees on each call (instructions, retrieved documents, memory, conversation history, tool results) and in what form, so it has exactly what it needs and nothing that distracts it.",
  "As applications grow from single prompts to agents and long conversations, quality depends less on clever wording and more on giving the model the right facts at the right time within a limited, costly context window.",
  ["The context window is limited and every token costs money and time.",
   "Retrieve just-in-time: fetch the documents, records or tool outputs needed for this step.",
   "Summarise or drop old history; keep key decisions and facts in a structured state or memory.",
   "Order matters: stable instructions first (also good for caching), then reference material, then the current question.",
   "Label each part clearly (tags or headings) and say how to use it ('answer only from the documents').",
   "For agents, keep tool outputs short and return only the fields the next step needs."],
  ["List every piece of information a perfect answer requires.",
   "Mark which parts are stable (rules), per-user (profile) and per-request (question, retrieved docs).",
   "Build a function that assembles the context from those parts with clear tags.",
   "Set a token budget for each part and trim to fit.",
   "Log the final context for each request so you can debug bad answers."],
  "def build_context(rules: str, profile: dict, docs: list[dict], history: list[str], question: str) -> str:\n    doc_text = '\\n'.join(f\"<doc source='{d['source']}'>{d['text']}</doc>\" for d in docs[:4])\n    recent = '\\n'.join(history[-6:])  # keep only the last few turns\n    return f'''<rules>{rules}</rules>\n<customer>name={profile['name']}; plan={profile['plan']}; city={profile['city']}</customer>\n<documents>\n{doc_text}\n</documents>\n<recent_conversation>\n{recent}\n</recent_conversation>\n<question>{question}</question>\nAnswer only from <documents>. If the answer is not there, say so and offer to connect a human.'''",
  ["Sending the entire conversation and all documents every time.",
   "Mixing untrusted document text with instructions without labels.",
   "Losing important early facts when truncating history.",
   "Not logging the context, so bad answers cannot be explained."],
  [("Context engineering vs prompt engineering?", "Prompt engineering focuses on how instructions are written; context engineering decides the whole set of information placed in the window for each call, including retrieval, memory, history and tool results."),
   ("How do you handle a conversation longer than the context window?", "Summarise older turns, store key facts in structured memory, retrieve relevant history when needed, or use provider features such as compaction."),
   ("Why put stable content first?", "It lets prompt caching reuse the prefix, reducing cost and latency, and keeps instructions prominent.")],
  "Instrument a chatbot to log context size per request, then cut average tokens by 40% without lowering scores on your evaluation set.",
  [("Anthropic: effective context engineering for AI agents", "https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"), ("Claude: prompt caching", "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/prompt-caching")]),

T("Embeddings",
  "An embedding is a vector (list of numbers) produced by an embedding model to represent the meaning of text, images or code. Items with similar meaning have vectors that are close together.",
  "Embeddings power semantic search, RAG, recommendations, clustering, duplicate detection and classification. They let you find 'cancel my plan' when the document says 'end your subscription'.",
  ["An embedding model reads text and outputs a fixed-length vector (for example 768 or 1,536 numbers).",
   "Similarity between two vectors is measured with cosine similarity (or dot product).",
   "To search: embed all documents once and store the vectors; embed each query and find the nearest vectors.",
   "Vector databases (pgvector, Pinecone, Qdrant) index vectors for fast nearest-neighbour search.",
   "Use the same embedding model for documents and queries; changing models means re-embedding everything.",
   "Providers include OpenAI, Google, Cohere, Voyage AI and open-source sentence-transformers models."],
  ["pip install sentence-transformers numpy (free, runs locally).",
   "Embed 10 FAQ answers.",
   "Embed a user question and compute cosine similarity to each FAQ.",
   "Return the best match and its score; test with paraphrased questions.",
   "Set a minimum score below which you answer 'I don't know'."],
  "# pip install sentence-transformers numpy\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\nimport numpy as np\n\nmodel = SentenceTransformer('all-MiniLM-L6-v2')  # small, free, local\nfaqs = [\n    'To end your subscription, go to Settings > Billing > Cancel plan.',\n    'Site visits can be booked from the property page or on WhatsApp.',\n    'Home loan pre-approval usually takes 3 to 5 working days.',\n]\ndoc_vecs = model.encode(faqs, normalize_embeddings=True)\n\ndef search(question: str, min_score: float = 0.35):\n    q = model.encode([question], normalize_embeddings=True)[0]\n    scores = doc_vecs @ q            # cosine similarity (vectors are normalised)\n    best = int(np.argmax(scores))\n    return (faqs[best], float(scores[best])) if scores[best] >= min_score else (None, float(scores[best]))\n\nprint(search('how do I cancel my plan?'))",
  ["Mixing vectors from different embedding models.",
   "Embedding whole long documents as one vector, which blurs meaning.",
   "No similarity threshold, so irrelevant matches are always returned.",
   "Forgetting to store the source text and metadata with the vector."],
  [("What is cosine similarity?", "The cosine of the angle between two vectors: 1 means same direction (very similar meaning), 0 unrelated. With normalised vectors it equals the dot product."),
   ("Keyword search vs semantic search?", "Keyword search matches exact terms (good for codes and names); semantic search matches meaning (good for paraphrases). Hybrid search combines both."),
   ("How do you choose an embedding model?", "Test retrieval quality on your own queries and documents, and weigh language support, vector size (storage and speed), price, hosting and licence.")],
  "Embed 100 support articles, build a search function, and measure how often the correct article is in the top 3 for 30 test questions.",
  [("OpenAI: embeddings guide", "https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/embeddings"), ("Sentence Transformers documentation", "https://www.sbert.net/"), ("Google: text embeddings", "https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/embeddings")]),

T("Tokenisation",
  "Tokenisation is splitting text into tokens, the units a language model reads and writes. A token is often a word piece: 'unbelievable' might be 'un', 'believ', 'able'. Models have limits and prices measured in tokens.",
  "Tokens decide cost, speed and whether your input fits the context window. Estimating tokens correctly is needed to budget projects, design chunking for RAG and avoid truncated outputs.",
  ["Each model family has its own tokenizer; the same text gives different counts on different models.",
   "English averages roughly three-quarters of a word per token, but numbers, code, and many non-English languages (including Indian languages) can use many more tokens.",
   "Input tokens (your prompt and context) and output tokens (the reply) are counted and priced separately; output usually costs more.",
   "The context window limits input plus output tokens per request.",
   "max_tokens (or a similar setting) caps the output length.",
   "Count exactly with the provider's tool: Anthropic's count_tokens endpoint, OpenAI's tiktoken library, Gemini's count_tokens."],
  ["Take a 1,000-word document and count its tokens with a provider tool.",
   "Compare counts for English, Hindi and a JSON sample.",
   "Calculate the cost of 10,000 such requests with the published prices.",
   "Decide a chunk size in tokens for RAG and a max output length for replies."],
  "# Estimate monthly cost from token counts (prices are placeholders: use the provider's pricing page)\nPRICE_IN_PER_M = 4.00    # $ per 1M input tokens\nPRICE_OUT_PER_M = 20.00  # $ per 1M output tokens\n\ndef monthly_cost(requests_per_day: int, in_tokens: int, out_tokens: int, days: int = 30) -> float:\n    per_request = in_tokens / 1e6 * PRICE_IN_PER_M + out_tokens / 1e6 * PRICE_OUT_PER_M\n    return round(per_request * requests_per_day * days, 2)\n\n# 2,000 chats a day, 3,000 input tokens (instructions + retrieved docs), 300 output tokens\nprint(monthly_cost(2000, 3000, 300))   # -> 1080.0\n\n# Exact counts (Claude):\n# client.messages.count_tokens(model='claude-opus-5-5', messages=[...]).input_tokens",
  ["Estimating cost from words instead of tokens.",
   "Forgetting that retrieved documents and conversation history count as input tokens on every call.",
   "Setting output limits so low that answers are cut off."],
  [("Why do non-English languages often cost more?", "Tokenizers trained mostly on English split other scripts into more, smaller tokens, so the same meaning needs more tokens."),
   ("How do you estimate the cost of an AI feature?", "Measure average input and output tokens per request on realistic samples, multiply by the price per token and by expected volume, and add retries, embeddings and evaluation runs."),
   ("What happens when input plus output exceeds the context window?", "The request is rejected or the output is cut short; you must trim, summarise or chunk the input.")],
  "Build a cost calculator sheet for an AI support bot: volume, tokens per call, model prices, cache hit rate, and monthly total for three models.",
  [("Claude: token counting", "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/token-counting"), ("OpenAI tiktoken (GitHub)", "https://github.com/openai/tiktoken"), ("Hugging Face: tokenizers summary", "https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/tokenizer_summary")]),

T("Basic RAG",
  "Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) answers questions by first retrieving relevant passages from your documents and then asking the model to answer using only those passages, with citations.",
  "Customers want AI that answers from their own policies, manuals, contracts and knowledge bases, which models were never trained on. RAG is the most common enterprise AI pattern and appears in most FDE interviews.",
  ["Ingest: load documents and extract clean text with metadata (source, page, date).",
   "Chunk: split text into passages of a few hundred tokens with some overlap.",
   "Embed and store: create a vector for each chunk and store it with text and metadata.",
   "Retrieve: embed the question and fetch the top-k most similar chunks (optionally filtered by metadata).",
   "Generate: put the chunks in the prompt with instructions to answer only from them and cite sources.",
   "Evaluate: test retrieval (right chunks found?) and answers (faithful and relevant?) on a question set."],
  ["Collect 5–10 real documents (PDF or text).",
   "Extract text and split into ~500-token chunks with 50-token overlap.",
   "Embed chunks and store them (a Python list for learning; pgvector or Qdrant for real use).",
   "For each question, retrieve the top 4 chunks.",
   "Send them to the model with grounding instructions and print the answer with sources.",
   "Create 20 test questions with expected sources and measure how often the right source is retrieved."],
  "# Minimal RAG: local embeddings + Claude for generation\nimport anthropic, numpy as np\nfrom sentence_transformers import SentenceTransformer\n\nemb = SentenceTransformer('all-MiniLM-L6-v2')\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()\n\ndef chunk(text: str, size: int = 120, overlap: int = 20) -> list[str]:\n    words = text.split()\n    return [' '.join(words[i:i + size]) for i in range(0, len(words), size - overlap)]\n\ndocs = {'leave_policy.txt': open('leave_policy.txt', encoding='utf-8').read()}\nchunks = [(src, c) for src, t in docs.items() for c in chunk(t)]\nvecs = emb.encode([c for _, c in chunks], normalize_embeddings=True)\n\ndef answer(question: str, k: int = 4) -> str:\n    q = emb.encode([question], normalize_embeddings=True)[0]\n    top = np.argsort(vecs @ q)[::-1][:k]\n    context = '\\n'.join(f\"<doc id='{i}' source='{chunks[i][0]}'>{chunks[i][1]}</doc>\" for i in top)\n    msg = client.messages.create(\n        model='claude-opus-5-5', max_tokens=1500,\n        system='Answer only from the documents. Cite doc ids like [doc 3]. If the answer is not in them, say you do not know.',\n        messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': f'<documents>{context}</documents>\\n<question>{question}</question>'}],\n    )\n    return ''.join(b.text for b in msg.content if b.type == 'text')\n\nprint(answer('How many days of paternity leave do employees get?'))",
  ["Chunks too large or too small; tune with real questions.",
   "No 'I don't know' instruction, so the model invents answers when retrieval fails.",
   "Ignoring document permissions, so users see content they should not.",
   "Never evaluating retrieval separately from generation."],
  [("Explain RAG end to end.", "Documents are parsed, chunked, embedded and stored with metadata. At query time the question is embedded, the nearest chunks are retrieved (often with filtering and reranking), and the model answers using only those chunks with citations. Retrieval and answer quality are evaluated separately."),
   ("RAG gives wrong answers. How do you debug it?", "Check whether the right chunks were retrieved. If not, fix parsing, chunking, embeddings, filters or add hybrid search/reranking. If yes, fix the prompt (grounding, citation rules) or model. Add failing cases to the test set."),
   ("When would you not use RAG?", "When the task needs behaviour or style change rather than facts (fine-tuning or prompting), when all needed data fits easily in the prompt, or when exact structured queries against a database are better.")],
  "Build a RAG assistant over a company's HR policies with citations and a 30-question evaluation, and report retrieval hit rate and answer faithfulness.",
  [("Pinecone: build a RAG chatbot", "https://docs.pinecone.io/guides/get-started/build-a-rag-chatbot"), ("Pinecone: chunking strategies", "https://www.pinecone.io/learn/chunking-strategies/"), ("Anthropic: contextual retrieval", "https://www.anthropic.com/news/contextual-retrieval"), ("Claude: citations", "https://docs.claude.com/en/docs/build-with-claude/citations")]),
]
